            "<strong>Delirium</strong> (acute confusional state) is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness with a physical cause. It affects a third or more of older people admitted to hospital, doubles mortality, lengthens stay, and is missed most of the time — especially the <strong>hypoactive</strong> form, in which the patient is quiet, drowsy and 'no trouble', rather than the hyperactive form in which she shouts, climbs out of bed and pulls out her lines. The <strong>80-year-old</strong> who was lucid at admission and is <strong>sudden</strong>ly confused on day two, who is calm by day and fighting <strong>each evening</strong>, who cannot count the months backwards and is <strong>pulling at her cannula</strong>, has delirium until proved otherwise. The <strong>first response</strong> is not haloperidol: it is to look for the cause. The mnemonic <strong>PINCH ME</strong> covers most: <strong>P</strong>ain, <strong>I</strong>nfection (urinary, chest, malaria, sepsis), <strong>N</strong>utrition (and thiamine), <strong>C</strong>onstipation, <strong>H</strong>ydration (and hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia, hypoxia), <strong>M</strong>edication (newly started, newly stopped, anticholinergics, sedatives, opioids, alcohol withdrawal), <strong>E</strong>nvironment (a strange ward, no glasses or hearing aid, no sleep, urinary retention, a full bladder). Glucose, temperature, saturation, urinalysis, a bladder scan or palpation, a rectal examination, the drug chart and a careful look for pain and injury find the cause in most cases.",
            "<strong>Managing the delirious patient</strong>: treat the cause; keep the same nurses and a calm, quiet, well-lit room by day and a dark quiet one at night; put her <strong>glasses and hearing aids</strong> in place; <strong>re-orientate</strong> gently and often — a clock, a calendar, a window, your name and the reason you are there; bring the <strong>family</strong> to the bedside, because a familiar face does what no drug can; mobilise, hydrate, feed, relieve pain and keep the bowel and bladder empty; protect sleep from night-time observations that are not needed; and remove what can be removed — the cannula she pulls at can be hidden under a bandage, resited or taken out if the infusion is finished. <strong>Restraint</strong> worsens delirium, causes injury and pressure sores and is used only where there is imminent danger and nothing else works, under the conditions of section 58. <strong>Sedation</strong> is reserved for severe distress or danger to the patient or others after the cause is being treated: a low dose of haloperidol, repeated cautiously; benzodiazepines only in alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, because elsewhere they deepen and prolong delirium; and never a <strong>sleeping tablet</strong> 'to settle her for the night'. Tell the family what delirium is — many believe she has 'gone mad' or been bewitched — and that it usually clears over days to weeks once the cause is treated, though some never fully return to baseline.",
