            "<strong>Dementia</strong> is a progressive decline in memory and at least one other cognitive domain (language, judgement, planning, recognition) severe enough to impair daily life, in clear consciousness. <strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong> is the commonest: insidious loss of recent <strong>memory</strong> — the <strong>forgetful</strong> grandfather who repeats questions, loses money and accuses others of stealing it, gets lost on a familiar path, cannot name grandchildren — followed by language, recognition and then everything else over years. <strong>Vascular dementia</strong> follows strokes and small-vessel disease in the hypertensive and diabetic, with a stepwise course, focal signs and preserved personality early. <strong>Lewy body dementia</strong> brings fluctuating cognition, vivid visual hallucinations, parkinsonism and a dangerous sensitivity to antipsychotics; <strong>frontotemporal dementia</strong> brings personality and behaviour change before memory loss; <strong>HIV</strong> causes a dementia that antiretrovirals improve. Before any dementia is accepted the <strong>reversible mimics</strong> are excluded: depression (the commonest), hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, normal pressure hydrocephalus, chronic subdural haematoma after a fall, syphilis, HIV, alcohol, drug toxicity and delirium itself.",
            "Assessment combines the history from the family (what changed, when, in what order, and what she can no longer do — cooking, money, dressing, continence), a <strong>cognitive screen</strong> (the Mini-Mental State Examination or a locally adapted, literacy-fair tool; orientation, three-word recall, attention, naming, drawing), the physical examination and bloods, and — gently — a check for depression, hearing and vision, which confound every test. A score is interpreted against the person's education and language: an unschooled 85-year-old farmer who cannot spell 'world' backwards is not demented for that reason.",
