            "<strong>Polypharmacy</strong> — five or more medicines, plus the herbal remedies and the market tablets nobody wrote down — is the commonest reversible cause of confusion, falls and admission in old age. Doses differ because kidney clearance falls even when the creatinine looks normal, body fat rises and water falls, and the ageing brain is more sensitive to anything that sedates or dries: <strong>start low, go slow, and review</strong>. The <strong>prescribing cascade</strong> turns one drug's side effect into the next drug's indication: the NSAID raises the blood pressure, the antihypertensive causes dizziness, the dizziness is treated with an antihistamine, the antihistamine causes confusion and retention, and the confusion is treated with haloperidol. The groups that repay a second look are <strong>sedatives and sleeping tablets</strong>, <strong>anticholinergic</strong> drugs (amitriptyline, chlorpromazine, antihistamines, drugs for the bladder — dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, retention, confusion), <strong>anti-inflammatories</strong>, antihypertensives and diuretics causing postural drops, and antidiabetics causing hypoglycaemia. <strong>Reviewing medicines at home</strong> means every container on the table, counting what is left, asking what she actually takes and which she skips and why — cost, the size of the tablet, the night-time dose that means getting up in the dark.",
